1b5bdd40499bd5c7417bdc532d52fef8847bdf3f
# ^ The Fairy-Stockfish commit the prod image builds for the Duck Xiangqi bot
# (railpack reads the first line; this comment is ignored). Deliberately the
# SAME upstream commit as fairy-stockfish-xiangqi.ref: our patch applies cleanly
# to it, so there is one upstream pin to move rather than two, and no fork to
# maintain (contrast pikafish-jieqi.ref, whose fork branch makes a bump a
# three-part change across two repos).
#
# Why a THIRD FSF binary exists. Stock Fairy-Stockfish cannot play Duck Xiangqi,
# and neither limitation is reachable from variants.ini:
#
#   1. MAX_MOVES is a compile-time constant, 1024 in a build without ALLVARS.
#      A turn here is a piece move AND a duck placement, so the opening array
#      alone generates 2,554 legal moves and random play peaks near 4,901.
#      Overflowing it is a stack smash inside MovePicker (its `moves` array is a
#      stack member) that presents as a SIGSEGV with a corrupted backtrace.
#   2. `wallingRule = duck` refuses a royal king, so the general is a non-royal
#      wazir, and FSF has no way to say what the flying general BECOMES once the
#      general is not royal. Upstream writes that rule as a prohibition in
#      `legal()` - a move leaving the generals facing is illegal - which is only
#      coherent while facing is self-check. With no check there is nothing for
#      it to rest on, so the patch adds the other half in `generate_all()`: a
#      general may fly down a clear file and CAPTURE the enemy general.
#
#      The prohibition itself is left exactly as upstream wrote it, still
#      guarded on `count<KING>()`. That guard is what keeps the two readings
#      from ever both applying: a royal-king variant (standard xiangqi, janggi)
#      is untouched, and only a non-royal general reaches the capture.
#
#      Revised 2026-09-10. Before that the patch forced the prohibition ON for
#      the non-royal case, and re-tested it after the duck's half of the turn.
#      That kept a consequence without its cause, and the kernel has since made
#      facing legal-and-losing to match Duck Chess, where kings may stand
#      adjacent and a king captures a king.
#
# Why not just patch the xiangqi binary and share one. The patch drops MAX_PLY
# from 246 to 100 to keep MovePicker (8192 * 8 = 64KB per ply) inside the 8MB
# thread stack. Quietly capping the search depth of an already-rated ladder is
# not a side effect worth having, so the xiangqi binary is left alone.
#
# The patch is fairy-stockfish-duck-xiangqi.patch, applied by railpack after
# checkout. It is part of this bot's identity as much as the ref is: a change to
# EITHER is a different engine, and DUCK_XIANGQI_FSF_ENGINE_REF carries a digest
# of both so a rated identity cannot silently change how it plays.
#
# The build is gated on THREE perft(1) counts, each the kernel's own answer for
# the same position, because a binary that plays a different game does not
# crash, it just plays a different game:
#
#   2554  the opening array. Catches a binary generating only the 44 piece
#         moves, ie. one that did not get the duck at all.
#    430  `9/4k4/9/9/9/4*4/P8/9/4K4/9 w - - 0 1` - generals on one file with the
#         DUCK the only thing between them. The opening count cannot see the
#         facing rule at all (it reads 2554 with `flyingGeneral` on or off), and
#         this position is what discriminates: the pre-2026-09-10 binary, which
#         confined the duck to the open segment, answers 187 here.
#    431  `9/4k4/9/9/9/9/P8/9/4K4/*8 w - - 0 1` - the same file with NOTHING
#         between. The extra move over a plain count is the flying capture
#         itself, so this fails if the movegen hunk is dropped while the
#         prohibition stays off, which is the silent third game: generals may
#         face and neither can do anything about it.
#
# No NNUE. The pinned net is a standard-xiangqi net; this variant runs classical
# eval, the same footing as xiangqi rungs 1-7.
#
# Bumping: change the SHA here, re-check the patch still applies, and re-run the
# EvE calibration so the bot ratings are measured on the new binary.
